Z||000000||PKFieroGT||02-23-2001||11:58 AM||||I have an '86 GT w/2.8 V6 that has a really hard time starting when it's cold. It's fine above the freezing mark, but when it gets to -10 degrees Celcius (sorry, don't know Fahrenheit Equivalent), It's a crap shoot if the car will start. I've replaced the wires, Cap and rotor and Ignition Coil, but I suspect it's a fuel problem. I usually have to wait to hear the fuel pump come on and click off before i can start it, or else it just keeps rotating until the pressure builds up. I last replaced my fuel filter 'bout a year and a half ago, and I've tried various grades of gas as a quick test, but there was no difference. The car will eventually start, but I usually drain my battery, and have to boost it with my wife's **groan** minivan  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><BR>Any help would be apprieciated...<P>------------------<BR>PK<BR>""Ï should join a club and beat you over the head with it!||||reg||10
Z||000001||87GTZ34||02-23-2001||12:05 PM||||How many miles on the eng?  I would start (no pun intended) with the cold-start injector.  Make sure it is clean & working and actually you may want to run some injector cleaner/lube anyway if you haven't done that.  Also, check for vacuum leaks on tubing and gaskets as leaning out an eng for cold starts doesn't help starting.||||reg||1
Z||000002||c m west||02-23-2001||12:24 PM||||try running a test to see if the cold start switch is activating||||reg||1
Z||000003||Indiana_resto_guy||02-23-2001||04:03 PM||||Simple ?<BR>Does it start OK when you let the pump run and then kick off?<BR>If it does then why not just let the pump build pressure before you start up?<BR>May be an indication of things to come . . .||||reg||1
Z||000004||PKFieroGT||02-23-2001||06:23 PM||||There are 130 000 Kilometers on my GT,(again, sorry for the metric) but I suspect there may be more... the guy i bought it off of was a bit of a scammer...<BR>Anyway, i have thought of the Cold start...What's the best way to check it?(ie easiest) <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>I do let the fuel pump build pressure, and wait until the relay clicks off, but it still happens. I'm running a thin oil too (5W30) for the winter, so I've eliminated that as well...<BR>Thanks to all that replied, I'll look into that Cols start injector this weekend!!!<BR><P>------------------<BR>PK<BR>""Ï should join a club and beat you over the head with it!||||reg||1
Z||000005||87GTZ34||02-25-2001||12:11 AM||||I had to look up some wiring info for my car today and thought I would look at the schematic for the cold start circuit.  It appears as though you can bypass the switch to test the system, and see if forcing the cold start on changes cold start behavior.  It is the double pole sensor on the right next to the water outlet on the eng.  Don't leave it jumpered for very long as normally it only stays on for a max duration of 8 seconds.  Hope this helps.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Indiana_resto_guy||02-25-2001||06:57 AM||||If you are still having this problem, try installing some fresh plugs. The ones that are in it may be old and worn out and possibly have an electrode or two broken internally. A;sp get some electrical contact clraner and clean the connections on you ingnition module.||||reg||1
